From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g17: restart the GPU firmware across a
 firmware-driven suspend

With no sleep callbacks, a suspend-to-RAM goes through with the firmware
running and loses it: an idle GPU reads actual state 0, so the sleep
driver's check passes, and the next submit after the wake times out.

macOS doesn't reload the firmware. Measured on T8140 under aura, it
idles GFX then GFX1, lowers the GFX power state, and after the wake
takes HELLO on GFX1 then GFX with no initialization, no endpoint starts
and no buffer requests, then sends POWER_ON_DONE on both. So rings and
firmware state stay in DRAM and only the RTKit handshake is repeated. Do
the same, in macOS's order and values:

- Down: GFX's idle, the gating word's bit 2, GFX1's idle, each
  instance's mailbox off after its IOP ack and its run bit 5 us later,
  the PMP's request for the GPU withdrawn, then automatic power
  management, the bridge words, and the target, polled until its busy
  bit clears. Both idle first, so a refusal by the second can still wake
  the first.
- Up: the target, the bridge, automatic power management, the PMP's
  request, gating, then GFX1's run bit and later GFX's.

The prepare callback closes publication, gives work in flight 3 s to
retire and refuses the sleep otherwise. It runs before any device
suspends. An idled firmware can't be woken with its power kept, so where
the sleep controller can take it, the down sequence runs in C as the
first of the sleep's last stops, after every refusal and with interrupts
off: both instances idled through RTKit's polled idle, each mailbox's
receive and run bit off, the PMP's request withdrawn while the PMP still
runs, then the bridge and the domain. Told to idle, the firmware can't
carry on, so the sleep goes on whatever it answers. .complete asks
whether that stop ran: if it didn't, the sleep was refused before the
commit, the firmware never stopped and publication reopens; if it did,
the books are settled without touching the registers and the wake's
restart follows. Elsewhere .prepare stops the GPU itself. A sleep
refused after a stop leaves the firmware stopped and publication closed,
but brings the domain back: userspace runs again during the hold, and
ioctls, debugfs and unbind can all reach what's behind it. GFX's domain
is driver-owned for the sleep, so the power-domain resync leaves it to
this order.

The GPU's OSLOG counters stay unanswered, as before: RTKit now echoes
every T8140 coprocessor's, and no soak has echoed the GPU's yet.

The ordering logic is a pure function over a hardware trait, with KUnit
tests.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
(cherry picked from commit c8515595061e8a74df49054af59226b484c15c07)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
